    challengeRating: 5,
    prompt: "Mara Velasco, CFA, runs an independent research boutique. A small-cap battery maker, Voltreon, asks her to write a sponsored initiation-of-coverage report that the company will distribute to investors. Voltreon offers three things: a fixed fee of 40,000 dollars payable on delivery regardless of her conclusion; warrants to buy 5,000 Voltreon shares that vest only if the report assigns a Buy rating; and a free seat at Voltreon's annual investor day. Mara intends to write a scrupulously honest report and plans to disclose, prominently on the cover, that Voltreon commissioned and paid for the research and that she received warrants. Under Standard I(B), which arrangement should Mara accept as structured?",
    correct: "Accept the fixed 40,000 dollar fee paid on delivery regardless of her conclusion, decline the rating-contingent warrants, and disclose that the research is issuer-paid.",
    wrong: [
      ["Accept all three items, including the warrants, because she prominently discloses on the report's cover that Voltreon paid her and granted her warrants, which cures the conflict.", "Treats disclosure as a cure-all. Compensation that vests only on a Buy rating is a conflict that must be AVOIDED, not disclosed. Disclosure lets a reader judge a residual conflict; it cannot neutralize a payment structure that pays Mara more for a favorable conclusion.", "Ask first whether the conflict can be eliminated. If pay rises with a positive rating, no cover-page sentence makes the analysis independent. Standard I(B) says limit compensation to a flat fee not tied to the conclusion, then disclose."],
      ["Accept the fixed fee and the warrants but write the report with extra rigor and a conservative tone, since her sincere intention to stay honest is what Standard I(B) actually protects.", "Chooses the most-correct intention over the most-correct action. The Standard judges the arrangement a reasonable investor would expect to compromise objectivity, not Mara's private resolve. Rating-contingent warrants give her a direct financial stake in a Buy regardless of how honest she feels.", "Independence is structural, not attitudinal. The fix is removing the equity stake tied to the rating, not promising yourself you will resist it. Compliance reviews defend the structure on the page, not the analyst's good faith."],
      ["Decline the entire engagement, because any compensation from the issuer being covered violates Standard I(B) and an analyst may never accept payment from a company she is researching.", "Forgets that issuer-paid research is permitted when properly structured. The Standard does not ban it; it requires a flat fee unlinked to conclusions plus disclosure. Refusing all issuer compensation overstates the rule and is not the action a compliance review would require.", "The line is not 'never take issuer money' but 'take only compensation that cannot reward a favorable view, and disclose it.' A flat delivery fee plus clear issuer-paid disclosure is the defensible middle, not a blanket refusal."],
    ],
    mentorHint: "Sort each payment by one question, does it pay more for a favorable conclusion: if yes, avoid it; if no, disclosing it is enough.",
    lesson: "The governing principle in Standard I(B) is that independence and objectivity are properties of the arrangement, not of the analyst's intentions. A documented compliance review asks one question: would a reasonable, knowledgeable third party expect this compensation structure to compromise the analyst's objectivity? Anything that answers yes must be avoided. Anything that survives the test but still carries a residual conflict gets disclosed. Disclosure is the second-best tool, reserved for conflicts you cannot eliminate; it is never a license to accept a conflict you could have removed.\n\nApply that to the three items. The fixed fee is paid on delivery regardless of conclusion, so it pays the same whether Mara writes Buy, Hold, or Sell. It does not reward a favorable view, so it survives the test and need only be disclosed as issuer-paid. The warrants are different in kind: they vest only on a Buy rating, which means Mara is literally paid more for a positive conclusion. That is the textbook example of compensation that could reasonably be expected to determine the content of the report, so it must be declined, not disclosed away. The investor-day seat is ordinary, modest business courtesy and is generally acceptable, though it should be disclosed and not the sort of lavish travel or accommodation the Standard warns against.\n\nNotice the two traps that look responsible. Writing the report 'extra carefully' while holding the warrants substitutes sincere intent for a clean structure, exactly the error the Standard is built to prevent. Walking away from the whole engagement overcorrects: issuer-paid research is explicitly permitted when the analyst limits compensation to a flat fee unconnected to the conclusion and discloses the relationship. The defensible action keeps the fee, kills the equity-linked kicker, and discloses, in that order.",
    solution: "Standard I(B), Independence and Objectivity, requires members and candidates not to offer, solicit, or accept any gift, benefit, compensation, or consideration that could reasonably be expected to compromise their own or another party's independence and objectivity. Note the test: it is the reasonable expectation of compromise, judged by an informed outside observer, not the analyst's self-assessed integrity.\n\nFor issuer-paid (sponsored) research, the Standard recognizes a real and significant conflict: the company paying for the report wants favorable coverage. The recommended procedure is to limit compensation to a flat fee, agreed before the work begins, that is not linked to the report's conclusions or recommendations, and to disclose to readers that the research is issuer-paid. Compensation tied to the outcome is prohibited because it can reasonably be expected to determine or affect the content. This includes both direct forms (a bonus for a Buy) and indirect forms (stock, warrants, options, or other equity instruments that gain value if the coverage is positive). Warrants that vest only on a Buy rating are squarely indirect, outcome-linked compensation and must be declined.\n\nBuild a hierarchy you can apply under exam pressure. First, AVOID conflicts you can eliminate by structure (here: drop the contingent warrants). Second, DISCLOSE the conflicts you cannot eliminate (here: that Voltreon commissioned and paid for the report). Disclosure never moves up to fill the role of avoidance. Two further anchors: gifts from clients differ from inducements from issuers or third parties, because a client already has a compensation relationship with you, so a client gift is closer to supplementary compensation (still disclose to your employer); and modest, customary business entertainment such as an investor-day seat is permissible, while lavish travel, paid airfare, or luxury accommodation from a covered issuer is not. Finally, when a securities law or firm policy is stricter than the Code, follow the stricter rule.",
    alternatePrompts: { plain: "A pharmaceutical micro-cap hires an independent CFA charterholder to produce a paid research note it will circulate to investors, offering a flat 30,000 dollar retainer due on completion, plus a 0.25 percent equity stake in the company that converts only if the note carries an Outperform rating, plus reimbursement for economy airfare to a site visit. The analyst plans to disclose every payment in a footnote. What combination of these terms is consistent with Standard I(B)?" },
