    id: 8492003,
    chapter: "Ethics Reflexes",
    title: "Mosaic vs. the one tainted fact",
    generated: true,
    challengeRating: 5,
    prompt: "Priya Anand, CFA, covers regional grocery chains. Over six weeks she builds a genuinely original buy thesis on Marrowbay Foods from public filings, satellite parking-lot counts, two supplier interviews about shelf-space gains, and her own channel checks — none of it material on its own, all of it legally gathered. The day before she plans to publish, a Marrowbay treasury employee she met at a conference texts her, unsolicited: \"Heads up, the board approved a 40% dividend hike, announcing Thursday — not out yet.\" This single fact is plainly material and not yet public. Priya is confident her published thesis would stand entirely on her independent mosaic work and never mentions the dividend. She drafts a detailed three-page memo to compliance documenting every public and nonmaterial source behind the thesis, arguing the dividend tip is irrelevant to a recommendation she could defensibly make without it, and proposes publishing the buy report on schedule before Thursday. What is the most appropriate action under Standard II(A)?",
    correct: "Do not trade or publish ahead of the announcement; report the tip to compliance, treat Marrowbay as restricted until the dividend news is public, and encourage the information to be disseminated — then act.",
    wrong: [
      ["Publish the buy report on schedule, relying on the three-page memo documenting that every input was public or nonmaterial, since the mosaic thesis stands independently of the dividend tip.", "This is the technically arguable but wrong call. Mosaic theory protects conclusions built only from public plus nonmaterial nonpublic pieces. Once Priya actually possesses a specific, material, nonpublic fact, she cannot 'un-know' it; trading or publishing a recommendation while in possession of that fact violates Standard II(A) no matter how clean the parallel mosaic file looks. The memo documents intent, not a defensible action.", "Ask whether the action would survive review by someone who knows she received the dividend tip — not whether her notes look independent."],
      ["Publish, but add a prominent disclosure in the report stating that she received an unsolicited nonpublic dividend tip which she did not rely on, so readers and regulators can judge for themselves.", "Disclosure is not a cure for material nonpublic information. Standard II(A) requires you to refrain from acting, not to confess and proceed. Announcing the tip while trading on it spreads the breach rather than containing it, and a clever disclosure is exactly the elaborate self-justification the rule forbids.", "Some conflicts must be avoided, not disclosed; MNPI is one of them."],
      ["Delete the text, ask the treasury employee never to contact her again, and publish on schedule, since she never solicited the tip and is therefore not responsible for possessing it.", "Whether the tip was solicited is irrelevant to whether she now possesses material nonpublic information — she does, and deleting the message destroys evidence rather than discharging the duty. The obligation is to not act and to report it, not to quietly dispose of it and proceed.", "Possession, not how you got it, triggers the duty; the fix is restraint plus reporting, not housekeeping."],
    ],
    mentorHint: "Once a single material, nonpublic fact lands in your hands, no amount of clean parallel research lets you trade around it — the duty is to stop and report, not to document your way through.",
    lesson: "The trap here is that Priya's mosaic work is real and admirable. Standard II(A)'s mosaic theory genuinely protects an analyst who reaches a material conclusion by combining public information with nonmaterial nonpublic pieces — that is legal, valuable analysis, and her satellite counts, supplier interviews, and channel checks are textbook mosaic inputs. The instinct to protect that work by papering a file proving its independence feels rigorous. But the scenario quietly slips one extra item into her possession that is, by itself, both material and nonpublic: a specific, unannounced board decision. The moment that arrives, the question is no longer about her mosaic at all.\n\nThe candidate error is believing that a sufficiently clean parallel record lets you trade through a tainted fact. It does not. You cannot partition your own brain, and the Standard prohibits acting on — or causing others to act on — material nonpublic information you possess, regardless of how you would have reached the same view otherwise. The elaborate three-page memo is the elegant-looking shortcut: it converts a simple prohibition (\"don't act, do report\") into a self-justification project. So does a flashy disclosure. The Standard's recommended procedures point the other way: refrain from trading, place the security on a restricted list, alert compliance, and encourage the issuer to disseminate the information publicly. Once Thursday's announcement makes the dividend public, the information is no longer nonpublic, and Priya's thesis — which never depended on it — can be published freely.\n\nNotice the difference between the wrong answer that confesses (disclosure) and the wrong answer that conceals (delete the text). Both are reactions to a duty that calls for neither cleverness nor cleanup, just restraint. The defensible action is boring, and that is the point: a documented compliance review wants to see that you stopped, not that you out-argued the rule.",
    solution: "Standard II(A), Material Nonpublic Information, sits inside Standard II, Integrity of Capital Markets. Its core rule has two halves. First, members and candidates who possess information that is both MATERIAL (a reasonable investor would want it before deciding, or it would move the price) and NONPUBLIC (not yet disseminated to the marketplace) must not act on it or cause others to act on it. Second, mosaic theory carves out a safe harbor: combining PUBLIC information with NONMATERIAL nonpublic information into a material conclusion is legitimate analysis, even if a company insider handing you that same conclusion directly would have been giving you inside information.\n\nThe skill the exam tests is sorting the inputs. Run each piece through two independent gates — material? and nonpublic? Only a piece that fails BOTH gates (material AND nonpublic) is poison. Public filings, satellite imagery, expert/supplier interviews about general trends, and your own channel checks are classic mosaic-safe inputs. A specific, unannounced, price-moving corporate action communicated to you before release is classic MNPI. Critically, the existence of clean mosaic inputs does not detoxify a separate MNPI item you also hold; possession is binary and you cannot trade 'around' it.\n\nWhen you find yourself holding MNPI, the recommended procedures are concrete and unglamorous: (1) do not trade and do not recommend the security; (2) notify your supervisor or compliance; (3) have the firm place the security on a restricted list and wall off the information; (4) encourage the issuer to make the information public. After public dissemination, the information loses its nonpublic status and you may act. Watch for three exam tells: an answer that uses disclosure as a substitute for refraining (disclosure does not cure MNPI), an answer that leans on solicited-versus-unsolicited (irrelevant — possession is what matters), and an answer that dresses up trading in a long mosaic memo (documentation of intent is not a defense). The most-correct ACTION beats the most-sincere INTENTION every time.",
    alternatePrompts: { plain: "An analyst has independently built a strong buy case on a stock using only public and nonmaterial sources, but the night before publishing she is unexpectedly told a specific, unannounced, price-moving fact by a company insider. She wants to publish her report — which does not rely on the tip — on schedule. Under the Code and Standards, which course of action is most defensible?" },
